<BaseLayout title="Evidence & corrections">
  <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
  <h1>The claim sets the standard.</h1>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What did the source actually establish?</h2>
    <p>
      An announcement can establish that a tool became available. A benchmark describes performance
      in a particular evaluation. Neither alone establishes benefit for patients. Regulatory action,
      external validation and independent evaluation answer different questions.
    </p>
    <h2>Read the setting and the limits</h2>
    <p>
      Research reporting identifies the design, population or dataset, comparator, endpoints,
      results, limitations and applicability. Missing information stays visible. Preprints,
      abstracts, trial registrations, results and peer-reviewed reports are labelled separately. We
      distinguish vendor claims from independent evidence and association from causation.
    </p>
    <h2>Fewer stories, clearer decisions</h2>
    <p>
      We select meaningful changes, track promising but incomplete developments, and reject
      duplicates or unsupported claims. There is no story quota per desk. A quiet day is a valid
      editorial outcome.
    </p>
    <h2>Corrections and accountability</h2>
    <p>
      Material corrections are dated and explained on the article. Retractions and serious source
      challenges prompt a correction or withdrawal assessment. The maintainer reviews the actual
      revision before publication; model assistance is not independent clinical validation.
    </p>
    <p>
      To report an error, <a href="/contact/">open a correction request</a> with the article and
      source details. Do not include patient or confidential information.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/GadDev/healthtech-ai-pulse/blob/main/EDITORIAL.md">
        Read the complete editorial policy.
      </a>
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
